// Where a finding's document becomes a row of numbers, or is found to
// be one already. The vector the scoring group reads its category
// counts off, computed from the document and the domain's term set and
// from nothing else, beside the integer that says what it is comparable
// with.
//
// THREE LIBRARIES ARE SPLICED HERE AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE TO EXPLAIN.
// src/lib/features.ts builds the vector and src/lib/feature-version.ts
// composes the number that dates it, which is the pair this group was
// planned around. src/lib/static-gate.ts is here for its hits alone:
// features.ts reads a gate result as a score AND the terms it found
// present, and the category columns are counts of those hits, one per
// category the domain's terms name. That block is the largest in the
// layout and most of what a vector says, and a vector built without it
// would store a block of zeros for every document in every domain. A
// zero there reads as a count somebody took, and the difference between
// a measured nothing and an absence is the distinction
// documents.features draws in its own column comment.
//
// Nothing on this canvas gates anything, so unlike ar-ingest's twin
// there is no earlier reading to take a score off even where that would
// be the right move. scoreText is a pure function of a body and a term
// set and both are read off nodes above, so the reading taken here is
// the reading the ingesting pass took, recomputed rather than
// remembered.
//
// MARKER ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. static-gate.ts and features.ts each
// declare a top-level function asText, which is a redeclaration the
// runtime accepts and the later one wins, so features.ts is spliced
// second on purpose: its version answers the empty string for a value
// whose string conversion throws, and static-gate's lets the throw out.
// The other order would quietly weaken the guard the library that lost
// wrote for itself. Nothing checks this. assertSpliceable in
// scripts/workflow-markers.ts judges each library alone, and a shadowed
// helper with a different body loads without complaint.
//
// THE SPEC IS THE TERM SET AND NOTHING ELSE. FeatureSpec takes a
// quantity roster and a one-hot roster beside the terms and both are
// empty below. DomainSettings in src/db/schema/domains.ts declares
// neither, so reading one off a domain would invent a key nobody wrote,
// and the version is the stronger reason: featureVersionFor digests the
// mechanism and the term set, so a roster arriving from anywhere else
// would move the layout under a number that cannot see it, which is the
// drift this pin exists for.
//
// WHY THE SKIP IS KEYED ON THE COMPOSED VERSION RATHER THAN ON THE
// MECHANISM VERSION ALONE. featureVersionFor answers the mechanism
// version multiplied by TERM_DIGEST_SPAN plus a digest over the
// domain's terms, so both halves survive in the one integer and either
// can be read back out of it: dividing a stored version by
// TERM_DIGEST_SPAN gives the mechanism the vector was computed under. A
// skip keyed on the mechanism alone is therefore a comparison this node
// could make. It does not make it.
//
// FEATURE_MECHANISM_VERSION moves when the extraction moves, and a
// developer moves it: a column kind added, a hit attributed
// differently, a text measure changed. That is the smaller half of what
// makes a vector mean something. The other half belongs to the domain
// and moves far more often, because an operator owns it. A term added
// or removed, a weight edited, a term re-filed under another category:
// each of those changes the layout or what lands in it, a category
// whose terms are all gone having no column at all and a hit whose
// pattern the term set no longer names being counted in the catch-all
// instead, while the mechanism version stays exactly where it was.
//
// So a skip keyed on the mechanism would leave every vector in a domain
// reading as current on the far side of the edit that made them all
// stale, and it would do it silently: a feature record is finite
// numbers, so comparing two of them succeeds whether or not the columns
// still stand for the same things. The reverse is equally true and is
// why the digest alone is not the key either, a term set nobody touched
// under an extraction that changed producing the same digest and a
// different vector. Both halves, or the number is not a version.
//
// WHAT A SKIP LEAVES ALONE. A document whose stored feature_version
// equals the composed one was read against this exact term set under
// this exact mechanism, so the vector on its row is what a
// recomputation would answer and the stored one travels instead of a
// fresh reading. It is used only where there is an object to use.
// Nothing in the database ties documents.features to
// documents.feature_version, which that column says out loud in its own
// comment, so a version stored with no vector beside it is a storable
// row and there is nothing there to reuse. Nothing else about the
// stored value is checked. Its keys are not held against featureKeys,
// because the version IS the claim that they match, and a second test
// keyed on the key list would be a competing mechanism that recomputed
// forever wherever the two disagreed.
//
// THE DOMAIN'S PIN IS READ AND IS NOT THE KEY. Load Domain Context
// carries domains.feature_version and this node holds the composed
// version against it, but the skip is decided on the document's own
// number. Nothing in this pipeline sets the domain's: ar-ingest writes
// every vector it computes at the composed version and leaves that
// column to the corpus-wide recompute that has not landed, so a domain
// featurized for months still reads NULL there and a skip keyed on it
// would recompute every vector on every pass. Once something does set
// it, it is a record of the recompute an operator ran rather than of
// what any particular vector was read against, which is the weaker of
// the two claims. It rides forward as feature_version_matches_domain
// for the reason ar-ingest carries the same member: an execution can
// then say whether the vectors this pass scored against are at the
// generation the domain claims, which is a fact about the domain a
// scoring pass can report and cannot repair.
//
// NOTHING HERE WRITES A COLUMN, and that is what separates this group
// from ar-ingest's. There a vector is computed for a document the pass
// has just inserted and the Postgres node under it stores the vector
// and its version together; here the vector is a reading the scoring
// group below consumes, over a corpus this workflow did not create. The
// consequence is worth naming rather than leaving to be discovered: a
// document that arrived through ar-capture carries no vector at all, so
// it is computed again on every pass that scores a finding over it.
// What would end that is not a node here. A recompute has to move
// domains.feature_version with it, in one statement over one domain,
// and a workflow scoring the findings one caller named would be
// claiming a corpus-wide pass it did not run.
//
// NULL VERSUS ZERO, which the vector answers differently from the score
// below it. A feature record cannot hold null: every cell is a finite
// number by construction, a null or a NaN being a cell that crashes
// whatever reads the matrix, so absence is carried by the layout rather
// than by a value, a quantity getting a companion known flag and a
// one-hot saying nothing was stated by having every member AND its
// catch-all at zero. features.ts argues both in full. The row-level
// half is the pair this node reads and does not write:
// documents.features is NULL for a document nothing has featurized,
// where an empty object would say a featurizer ran and measured
// nothing. What this node emits is never that empty record, the layout
// always naming the gate score and the two text columns, and
// feature_version is never 0 because featureVersionFor refuses a
// mechanism version below 1.
//
// AN EMPTY BODY IS TWO THINGS AND THE VECTOR CANNOT PART THEM, which is
// what document_parse_status travels for. A document whose parse failed
// still has a row and may still have an empty body, and the text
// columns read it at length zero exactly as they read a document that
// was fetched and found empty. So the status rides beside the vector
// rather than being folded into it, which is what Select Scoring
// Subjects says its projection is for. Nothing here refuses on it: a
// failed parse is still a document, its stored text is still what the
// corpus holds, and a reading taken over it is a reading of what is
// there.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in, one item out, and the item is a FINDING
// rather than a document. Select Scoring Subjects answers one row per
// finding the caller named and every group below is built on that, so
// this node pairs where ar-ingest's twin gathers. Two findings naming
// one document is the case worth stating: the vector is a function of
// the body and the term set alone, so both would answer the same
// numbers, and the map below computes each document once for the pass
// and hands that one record to both. It is a cost decision rather than
// a correctness one, and recomputing would be identical.
//
// WHAT DOES NOT TRAVEL PAST THIS NODE. The document body stops here.
// The vector is the reading taken over it, the scoring group below
// combines readings rather than text, and the node under that binds its
// item whole as one jsonb parameter, so a body carried two hops further
// would be every document of the pass shipped back to the database as a
// parameter. A node below that needs the text reaches
// $('Select Scoring Subjects').all() by position, this group being one
// item in and one out in the order it received them. The stored vector
// and the stored version stop here too: what replaces them is the
// answer below, and a reader holding both pairs at once would have to
// decide which of them was the vector.
//
// THE VERSION IS COMPOSED BEFORE ANY DOCUMENT IS READ.
// featureVersionFor refuses a term set it cannot read, member by
// member, so a domain whose rows are malformed stops the pass by name
// rather than scoring findings against vectors pinned to nothing.
// Composing it first is also what lets it be composed once: it is a
// fact about the domain, and every document in the pass is compared
// against the same one.

// The quantity roster and the one-hot roster this domain declares, both
// empty and both named rather than written inline so the paragraph
// above has somewhere to point. A domain that grows either one grows it
// in src/db/schema/domains.ts first, and the digest that pins the
// layout has to reach it before a reader here does.
const DECLARED_QUANTITIES = [];
const DECLARED_ONE_HOTS = [];

// The domain row and its taxonomy, read back off the node that fetched
// them. Select Scoring Subjects replaced the context item with its own
// answer, so a name is the only handle left.
const context = $('Load Domain Context').first().json;
const domain = context.domain;

// A hand over that resolved to no domain. Select Scoring Subjects
// selects on that domain and answers no rows at all when it is null,
// and a node handed no item does not run, so this is a backstop rather
// than a path: what it stops is a later selection above this one whose
// predicate is not the same.
if (domain === null || domain === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-score] Compute Feature Vectors has no domain row: the item '
    + 'this pass was handed resolves to none',
  );
}

// The layout, built once for the pass. The terms arrive with their
// category key already joined on, which is what makes one list serve
// three readers: static-gate.ts takes the pattern, the weight and the
// polarity, features.ts takes the pattern and the category, and
// feature-version.ts takes all four. Load Domain Context selects them
// that way and says so.
const terms = context.terms;
const spec = {
  terms,
  quantities: DECLARED_QUANTITIES,
  oneHots: DECLARED_ONE_HOTS,
};

// The composed version, and the term set's own validation, both before
// any document is read. One number for the whole pass, because it is a
// fact about the domain rather than about a document.
const version = featureVersionFor(FEATURE_MECHANISM_VERSION, terms);

// The domain's pin, held against what this configuration composes to.
// Read for the report and never for the skip, for the reason the header
// gives.
const pinned = domain.feature_version;
const matchesDomain = typeof pinned === 'number' && pinned === version;

// One vector per document for the whole pass, so two findings naming
// one document are one computation. Allocated here rather than beside
// the libraries because a Code node body runs once per execution, and a
// map that outlived one would be a cache shared between passes over
// different domains.
const vectors = new Map();

// The vector for one row: the stored one where the version says it is
// current, and a fresh reading otherwise. The answer says which of the
// two it is, because that is the one thing a reader cannot work out
// from the numbers.
//
// The stored value has to be a JSON object to be reused. A jsonb array
// and the JSON null literal both answer object to typeof, and neither
// is a feature record.
//
// The body is read as the corpus holds it and anything that is not text
// reads as the empty document it is. scoreText over that body and the
// domain's terms is the gate reading features.ts wants, and its hits
// are what the category block counts.
function vectorForDocument(row) {
  const stored = row.document_features;
  const storedVersion = row.document_feature_version;
  const current = typeof storedVersion === 'number'
    && storedVersion === version
    && stored !== null
    && typeof stored === 'object'
    && !Array.isArray(stored);

  if (current) {
    return { features: stored, source: 'stored' };
  }

  const body = typeof row.document_body === 'string'
    ? row.document_body
    : '';
  const scored = scoreText(body, terms);

  return {
    features: extractFeatures({ text: body }, scored, spec),
    source: 'computed',
  };
}

// One item per finding, in the order Select Scoring Subjects answered
// them.
return $input.all().map((item) => {
  const row = item.json;

  // A finding naming no document. findings.document_id is NOT NULL
  // behind a foreign key and the node above joins documents on it, so
  // an absence here is the canvas contradicting itself rather than a
  // state to handle. It is worth stopping for because nothing below
  // would: a vector keyed on undefined would be computed once and
  // handed to every such finding in the pass.
  if (row.document_id === null || row.document_id === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      '[ar-score] Compute Feature Vectors was handed a finding with no '
      + 'document id, which Select Scoring Subjects joins documents on',
    );
  }

  // The memo, keyed on the id as text so a bigint arriving as a string
  // and one arriving as a number are one document. Both spellings reach
  // this canvas: an id projected as a top-level column comes back as a
  // string and one taken out of a jsonb_agg array comes back as a
  // number.
  const documentKey = String(row.document_id);
  const cached = vectors.get(documentKey);
  const vector = cached === undefined
    ? vectorForDocument(row)
    : cached;

  vectors.set(documentKey, vector);

  // The finding, its criteria and its vector. The body, the stored
  // vector and the stored version are the members that stop here, for
  // the reason the header gives.
  return {
    json: {
      finding_id: row.finding_id,
      domain_id: row.domain_id,
      document_id: row.document_id,
      entity_id: row.entity_id,
      fields: row.fields,
      score: row.score,
      score_version: row.score_version,
      criteria: row.criteria,
      document_parse_status: row.document_parse_status,
      features: vector.features,
      feature_version: version,
      feature_vector_source: vector.source,
      feature_version_matches_domain: matchesDomain,
    },
  };
});

