// Where the readings this pass took become the one number a digest
// orders findings by. One item per finding in and one out, and what the
// item gains is a score with the version that dates it, or a null with
// an account of why there was nothing to answer.
//
// TWO LIBRARIES ARE SPLICED HERE AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE TO EXPLAIN.
// src/lib/aggregate-score.ts is the arithmetic and the whole of it: it
// reads no table, holds no signal name of its own, and combines
// whatever measurements it is handed under whatever scheme it is
// handed. src/lib/features.ts is here for two exported functions and
// for nothing else. asText and asKey are what turn a category key into
// the column the vector filed that bucket's count under, and that
// transform belongs to the library that built the record rather than to
// a second spelling of it in this node: categories.key is free text, so
// a mirror written here would look for a column that does not exist the
// first time an operator writes a key with a capital or a space in it,
// and would do it silently. The two libraries share no top-level name,
// measured over both sources, so the order they are spliced in is free
// rather than load-bearing.
//
// WHAT A SIGNAL IS ON THIS CANVAS. A domain states its position as
// criteria rows, each filed under one of its categories, and it states
// what each bucket is worth in domains.settings.scoringWeights. The
// reading for a bucket is the count features.ts put in that bucket's
// column, taken over the document behind the finding by the node above.
// So the scheme this node assembles is one part per category the domain
// has stated a criterion under, its share read off the weights under
// the name the operator wrote, and its measurement read out of the
// vector.
//
// WHAT A CRITERION'S KIND MEANS FOR A SCORE, which the two nodes above
// leave to this one to state. It means nothing to this arithmetic, and
// that is a statement rather than an omission. criteria.kind is free
// text with no CHECK, because which distinctions are worth drawing is
// part of what a domain states, so there is no closed vocabulary for a
// deterministic scorer to branch on and inventing one here would fix in
// code the thing that column refuses to fix in DDL. Its own comment
// says where a kind does belong: beside the value wherever criteria are
// handed to a model, and never used to filter them. Nothing on this
// canvas hands one to anything. So a criterion selects its bucket
// whichever way it points, and what a domain draws a distinction with
// instead is the share it gives each bucket and the terms it files
// under each.
//
// NULL AND ZERO ARE DIFFERENT NUMBERS, and this node is where the
// difference is made rather than where it is stated. A bucket whose
// column the vector carries is measured whatever the count says, so a
// count of nothing there is a measured zero: the domain's terms were
// run over the document and matched none of them. A bucket whose column
// the vector does not carry is unmeasured, and the layout is what says
// which is which, features.ts giving a category a column when a term
// names it and not otherwise. So a bucket the domain asks about and
// gives the pipeline nothing to match it with has no cell to read at
// all. The first is 0, the second is absent, and toFinite in
// aggregate-score.ts is what keeps them apart.
//
// HOW ABSENCE IS DECLARED, which aggregate-score.ts calls the
// substantive decision in a scoring scheme and gives no default for.
// counts-zero keeps a part's share in the denominator and contributes
// nothing to the sum, so an unmeasured bucket drags the total down.
// renormalizes drops it out of both, so the remaining shares carry the
// whole score. Every part here counts its absence as zero, because a
// criterion is the domain saying this bucket matters: a finding whose
// evidence touches none of the buckets that were asked about is a
// finding little was found about, which is a reading rather than a gap.
// The other answer is also not available on its own. aggregateTotal
// reports a record as unscored when every counts-zero part is
// unmeasured, so a scheme holding no such part can only ever answer
// null.
//
// NO PENALTY IS DECLARABLE TODAY. A penalty needs a coefficient and a
// scale beside its key, and DomainSettings.scoringWeights is a flat map
// of numbers with nowhere to put either, so the list below is empty and
// a domain has no way to state one. Where they would be declared is
// src/db/schema/domains.ts, beside the weights and under a shape of
// their own. The arithmetic is already here and applies them in the
// order they are listed, floating-point multiplication not being
// associative.
//
// THREE WAYS A PASS MEASURES NOTHING, all three answering null rather
// than zero. A domain that has stated no criteria has no part to score
// against, and a scheme with no parts is unscored by construction. A
// domain whose stated buckets are named by none of its terms has parts
// and no cells to read them out of. And a domain that has stated no
// share for any of them has a denominator of nothing, which
// aggregateTotal refuses rather than dividing by. Each of the three is
// a fact about the domain's configuration rather than about the
// finding, and each leaves the finding's two columns exactly as they
// were.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SCORED. The gate score, the two text-shape
// columns and the catch-all category are in every vector this pipeline
// builds, and none of them is a bucket a domain files a criterion
// under, so none of them has a share to carry. A domain that wanted one
// weighted would be naming a signal its own taxonomy does not hold,
// which is the invented key domains.settings refuses to be a home for.
// What admits it instead is a row: a category in categories with a
// criterion under it and terms filed against it, which is an operator's
// edit rather than a change here.
//
// THE VERSION IS THE MECHANISM'S AND NOT THE DOMAIN'S, which is the
// opposite of the pin the node above composes and is the schema's
// choice rather than this node's. findings.score_version says it in as
// many words: scoring is one mechanism over a domain's criteria rather
// than a per-domain pinned artifact, so what dates a score is the
// arithmetic that produced it. The constant below is where that number
// lives, and moving it is a developer's act belonging with a change to
// what a part means, to how a measurement is read, or to which column a
// bucket is read out of.
//
// WHAT THAT VERSION DOES NOT COVER, said here because nothing else says
// it. A weight edited in scoringWeights, a criterion added or
// withdrawn, a term re-filed under another category: each one changes
// every reading this node takes from then on, and none of them moves
// this number, so a re-score keyed on the version alone will not find
// those rows stale. benchmark_cases in src/db/schema/runs.ts names that
// exact drift and exists so a change to a domain's judgement can be
// measured rather than assumed. A composed version of the kind
// feature-version.ts builds for a vector would close the gap instead,
// and there is no such library today.
//
// THE PAIR LEAVES THIS NODE TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL. score_version
// carries the constant where there is a score and null where there is
// not, so the two members are one decision made in one place.
// findings.score_version asks for exactly that: a score stored with a
// null version reads as scored to everything looking for a number, with
// no version for a re-score to find it stale by, so it survives every
// pass the pin exists to trigger. The statement below guards on both
// members as well, which is a backstop rather than the rule.
//
// A TOTAL THAT IS NOT A FINITE NUMBER IS NOT A SCORE. aggregateTotal
// answers null for a record nothing measured, which is the ordinary
// outcome above, and can answer an infinity where a large enough
// measurement meets a large enough penalty, which aggregate-score.ts
// keeps deliberately because the design it ports answered it. Neither
// is written. Nothing on this canvas can produce the second today, no
// penalty being declarable, so the guard is against the shape rather
// than against a case anyone has met.
//
// THE TOTAL IS A WHOLE NUMBER, which is the arithmetic's contract
// rather than a claim about a range. A domain whose shares describe a
// zero-to-one score will find every total rounded to 0 or 1, which is a
// mismatch between the scale a domain wrote its weights on and the
// scale the rounding expects. aggregate-score.ts records it as the
// port's open scale question. findings.score is numeric and holds
// either, so nothing here refuses a domain that picked the smaller one.
//
// CARDINALITY, AND WHAT STOPS HERE. One item per finding in the order
// they arrived, so the node below pairs against this one by position
// rather than by a key. The vector, the criteria and the finding's
// fields payload all stop here: the score is what was made of them, and
// the statement below binds its item whole as one jsonb parameter, so a
// vector carried one hop further would be every document of the pass
// shipped back to the database as a parameter. The ids travel for the
// group that closes this run, and so does the domain pin the node above
// read.

// The scoring mechanism's own version, and the whole of what
// findings.score_version records. The header says what it covers and
// what it does not.
const SCORE_MECHANISM_VERSION = 1;

// The prefix features.ts gives its derived category columns. Named here
// rather than read out of the spliced library, whose own constant is
// private: a node reaching past what a library exports takes a
// dependency nothing reports and nothing can break loudly.
const CATEGORY_COLUMN_PREFIX = 'category_';

// The penalties this domain states, which is none of them. The header
// says where they would be declared and why there is nowhere yet.
const DECLARED_PENALTIES = [];

// The domain row and its settings, read back off the node that fetched
// them. Select Scoring Subjects replaced the context item with its own
// answer and Compute Feature Vectors replaced that in turn, so a name
// is the only handle left.
const context = $('Load Domain Context').first().json;
const domain = context.domain;

// A hand-over that resolved to no domain. The selection two nodes up
// answers no rows at all when it is null, and a node handed no item
// does not run, so this is a backstop rather than a path: what it stops
// is a later selection above this one whose predicate is not the same.
if (domain === null || domain === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-score] Aggregate Finding Scores has no domain row: the item '
    + 'this pass was handed resolves to none',
  );
}

// Whatever arrived, where a JSON object is what a reader needs and
// anything else is nothing. A jsonb array and the JSON null literal
// both answer object to typeof and neither is a map of shares or a
// vector.
function objectOrEmpty(value) {
  return value !== null
    && typeof value === 'object'
    && !Array.isArray(value)
    ? value
    : {};
}

// The domain's shares, keyed by the category key an operator wrote.
// Absent for a domain that has stated none, which is one of the three
// ways a pass measures nothing.
const weights = objectOrEmpty(objectOrEmpty(domain.settings).scoringWeights);

// The scheme for one finding: one part per category the finding's
// criteria are filed under, its share read off the weights under that
// same category key, and its absence counting as zero for the reason
// the header gives. The key is trimmed first, because the layout trims
// a category before it keys one, so a share and a column are looked up
// under the same spelling.
//
// Deduplicated on the operator's spelling and sorted, so two passes
// over one unchanged domain sum the same terms in the same order.
// aggregate-score.ts says the parts' order is part of the answer rather
// than a detail of the loop, floating-point addition not being
// associative.
//
// A share is read through Object.hasOwn rather than by indexing, and
// what that buys is the read rather than the number: every member of
// Object.prototype coerces through orZero to no share, so a category
// named after one answers 0 whichever spelling is used, measured. The
// guard is what makes the answer a reading of what the domain wrote
// rather than of what an object inherits. Its twin below, over the
// measurements, is the one that does change an answer.
//
// A category the domain stated no share for carries none, which leaves
// it in the count of what was asked about and out of the total.
function specFor(criteria) {
  const stated = [];
  const seen = new Set();
  const list = Array.isArray(criteria)
    ? criteria
    : [];

  for (const criterion of list) {
    const category = asText(objectOrEmpty(criterion).category).trim();

    if (category === '' || seen.has(category)) {
      continue;
    }

    seen.add(category);
    stated.push(category);
  }

  stated.sort();

  return {
    parts: stated.map((category) => ({
      key: category,
      weight: Object.hasOwn(weights, category)
        ? orZero(weights[category])
        : 0,
      absent: 'counts-zero',
    })),
    penalties: DECLARED_PENALTIES,
  };
}

// The measurements, keyed as the parts are. A bucket whose column the
// vector carries is measured whatever the count; a bucket whose column
// it does not carry is absent, and absent is what this record says by
// not holding the key at all.
//
// The column name is composed exactly as features.ts composes it, out
// of the two functions spliced in beside this node: the roster it
// builds its layout from trims a category and then keys it, and so does
// this.
//
// Two category keys that key alike share one column, which features.ts
// records as the collision its layout collapses. They stay two parts
// here and each carries its own share of that one count, the domain
// having stated two buckets and the vector holding one cell.
//
// Built on a null-prototype object so a category named after something
// on Object.prototype is an own key here. aggregateTotal reads a
// measurement as a plain property and says so, which on an ordinary
// object would make such a part read the prototype rather than nothing.
function measurementsFrom(features, parts) {
  const vector = objectOrEmpty(features);
  const measured = Object.create(null);

  for (const part of parts) {
    const column = CATEGORY_COLUMN_PREFIX + asKey(asText(part.key).trim());

    if (Object.hasOwn(vector, column)) {
      measured[part.key] = vector[column];
    }
  }

  return measured;
}

// One item per finding, in the order Compute Feature Vectors answered
// them.
return $input.all().map((item) => {
  const row = item.json;
  const spec = specFor(row.criteria);
  const measured = measurementsFrom(row.features, spec.parts);
  const total = aggregateTotal(measured, spec);

  // What the two columns get. A total that is not a finite number is
  // not a score, and the version goes with it: null and null, or a
  // number and the constant. The header says why the pair cannot be
  // parted.
  const scored = typeof total === 'number' && Number.isFinite(total);

  // How much of the domain's stated position the answer rests on.
  // Neither number reaches a column, findings having nowhere to put an
  // account of a score, so both ride as far as the group that closes
  // this run: the pair is what tells a domain that has configured
  // nothing apart from a corpus that matched nothing.
  const signalsStated = spec.parts.length;
  const signalsMeasured = spec.parts.filter(
    (part) => toFinite(measured[part.key]) !== null,
  ).length;

  // The finding and its score. The vector, the criteria and the fields
  // payload stop here, for the reason the header gives.
  return {
    json: {
      finding_id: row.finding_id,
      domain_id: row.domain_id,
      document_id: row.document_id,
      entity_id: row.entity_id,
      score: scored
        ? total
        : null,
      score_version: scored
        ? SCORE_MECHANISM_VERSION
        : null,
      score_signals_stated: signalsStated,
      score_signals_measured: signalsMeasured,
      feature_version: row.feature_version,
      feature_version_matches_domain: row.feature_version_matches_domain,
    },
  };
});
